    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build passes: **262 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, exit 0**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI made all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`, while keeping the HTTP status codes unchanged.\n\nThere were two producers of these rejections:\n\n**1. Controller-level rejections (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`)** \u2014 these previously returned bare, bodyless `ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.X)`. I replaced each of the 400/409/429 cases (invalid telephone, invalid/disposable email, missing address, invalid postcode, future registration date, daily-limit 429, city-capacity 409, duplicate-identity 409, and the two update-path 400s) with `throw new ResponseStatusException(status, \"<detail>\")`, giving each a meaningful detail message. The 404 (NOT_FOUND) paths were left untouched, as the spec only covers 400/409/429.\n\n**2. The global advice (`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`)** \u2014 I added a `@ExceptionHandler(ResponseStatusException.class)` that renders these into a `ProblemDetail` (reusing the existing `detailBuild` helper, so it carries `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail` plus the existing `timestamp`/`schemaValidationErrors`). I also explicitly set `Content-Type: application/problem+json` on every `ProblemDetail` response (including the pre-existing bean-validation 400 path), which is what the new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` asserts.\n\nSince `@ExceptionHandler` picks the most specific match, the new `ResponseStatusException` handler takes precedence over the catch-all `Exception` \u2192 500 handler, so these rejections keep their intended status.",
